The pattern, verified shape:

```jsx
import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';

// THE STRUCTURE: Arrange → Act → Assert
test('submitting the form calls save with the entered data', async () => {
  const save = jest.fn();                       // mock boundary
  render(<SignupForm onSave={save} />);          // Arrange: mount

  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/email/i), 'bo@x.com');  // Act: as a USER
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /submit/i }));

  expect(save).toHaveBeenCalledWith(             // Assert on BEHAVIOR
    expect.objectContaining({ email: 'bo@x.com' })
  );
});
```

What to test (the priority order):
1. User-visible behavior: interactions produce expected output — the form submits, the toggle switches, the error appears
2. State transitions the user SEES: loading → data → error states of a fetch hook/component
3. Edge cases in behavior: empty list renders 'no items', validation rejects bad input

What NOT to test (the discipline that separates seniors):
- NOT internal state (useState values) — test what the state CAUSES, not the state itself
- NOT implementation details (did the component call setX vs a reducer, class vs function)
- NOT snapshot-of-markup — breaks on every refactor, catches no real bugs

The query philosophy (RTL's core idea): query the way a USER finds things — by role (button, textbox), by label (form fields), by text. getByRole('button', {name: 'Submit'}) works regardless of markup changes; a CSS-selector query breaks when you rename a class. Accessibility-faithful queries also catch a11y bugs for free (a button no screen reader can find, a query can't find either).

The async mechanics (the practical details): everything after an interaction that triggers async (fetch resolving, a transition) needs `await` + `findBy*` queries (findByText = getByText + waitFor — retries until timeout). userEvent (user.type, user.click) over fireEvent: it fires the full real event sequence (focus → keydown → keypress → input), catching what fireEvent's single synthetic event misses.
